{Spin2_v51}
'' =================================================================================================
''
''   File....... isp_hub75_morphColor.spin2
''   Purpose.... Shared color helper for the morphing digits: one gamma curve and per-color intensity ramps
''   Authors.... Stephen M Moraco
''               -- see below for terms of use
''   E-mail..... stephen@ironsheep.biz
''   Started.... Oct 2026
''   Updated.... 14 Oct 2026
''   Version.... v1.0.0
''
'' =================================================================================================

{ Every partial-brightness pixel (a tail, an edge, anti-aliasing) is drawn at an intensity from 0
  (off) to 255 (the full color). The driver applies no gamma, so equal steps of raw level do not look
  like equal steps of brightness. This object turns an intensity into the packed bytes the driver
  stores, through a perceptual curve, with one table read per pixel.

  The gamma curve is in DAT, one copy for the program: every object that includes this file shares
  it, and it is built once. Keep this object free of CON overrides: an override that changed its
  image would give that instance its own DAT and a second curve (p2kb p2kbSpin2ObjectImageDedup).

  Usage:
    1. buildRamp(@ramp, chainIdx, rgbColor) - once per color, when a group is placed: fills the
                                              caller's RAMP_LEVELS LONGs (the curve is built on
                                              first use)
    2. packed := ramp[intensity]            - per pixel: the packed color at that intensity, ready for
                                              screenUtils.copyRowRun() or fillRowRun()

  The math. The color's channels are the levels the driver shows at full intensity, so they are
  kept exactly: each channel is scaled by the same perceptual factor,
      channel(i) = round(channel x curve(i)),   curve(i) = (i / 255) ^ GAMMA
  and packed through the driver's own packedCorrectedRgb(). So ramp[255] is the driver's packing
  of the full color, ramp[0] is black, the hue is the same at every level, and a channel that is 0
  in the color is 0 at every level. The curve is kept as 16-bit fractions so a channel is rounded
  once, not twice: an 8-bit curve would first round the factor, then the product.
}

CON ' ramp shape

  RAMP_LEVELS = 256                                             ' intensities 0 (off) to RAMP_MAX (full color): a caller's ramp is this many LONGs
  RAMP_OFF = 0                                                  ' the intensity of black
  RAMP_FIRST_LIT = RAMP_OFF + 1                                 ' the lowest intensity above black
  RAMP_MAX = RAMP_LEVELS - 1                                    ' the intensity of the full color

CON ' gamma curve

  GAMMA = 2.2                                                   ' the exponent of the perceptual curve (float)
  CURVE_FULL = 65_535                                           ' curve(RAMP_MAX): factors are 16-bit fractions of this
  CURVE_HALF = CURVE_FULL / 2                                   ' added before dividing by CURVE_FULL, so the channel rounds to nearest

OBJ

    pixels      : "isp_hub75_screenUtils"
    colorUtils  : "isp_hub75_colorUtils"

DAT ' shared gamma curve (one copy for the program; see the notes above)

  curveBuilt      long    FALSE                                 ' TRUE once buildCurve() has filled the curve
  curve           word    0[RAMP_LEVELS]                        ' curve(i) x CURVE_FULL, rounded

PUB null()
'' This is not a top level object
''

PUB buildCurve() | levelIdx
'' Build the shared gamma curve, once. Later calls return at once. buildRamp() calls this, so a
'' caller needs it only to take the start-up cost at a time of its choosing.
''

' Local Variables:
' @local levelIdx - the intensity whose curve value is being computed

    if curveBuilt <> TRUE
        curve[RAMP_OFF] := 0                                    ' POW of 0.0 is not used: log2(0) has no value
        repeat levelIdx from RAMP_FIRST_LIT to RAMP_MAX
            curve[levelIdx] := round(float(CURVE_FULL) *. ((float(levelIdx) /. float(RAMP_MAX)) POW GAMMA))
        curveBuilt := TRUE

PUB curveAt(intensity) : curveValue
'' Return the gamma curve's factor at an intensity, as a fraction of CURVE_FULL. For tests and
'' tuning; drawing reads a ramp, never the curve.
''
'' @param intensity - 0 to RAMP_MAX (only the low 8 bits are used)
'' @returns curveValue - the curve at that intensity, 0 to CURVE_FULL

    buildCurve()
    curveValue := curve[intensity & RAMP_MAX]

PUB buildRamp(pRamp, chainIdx, rgbColor) | levelIdx, red, green, blue, factor
'' Fill an intensity ramp for one color on one adapter: LONG[pRamp][level] is the packed color at
'' that intensity level, from 0 (black) to RAMP_MAX (exactly the driver's packing of rgbColor).
''
'' @param pRamp - hub address of RAMP_LEVELS LONGs, owned by the caller
'' @param chainIdx - the index of the adapter the ramp is drawn on (its color table packs the bytes)
'' @param rgbColor - the full color, $RRGGBB

' Local Variables:
' @local levelIdx - the intensity being filled
' @local red - the color's red channel at full intensity
' @local green - the color's green channel at full intensity
' @local blue - the color's blue channel at full intensity
' @local factor - the curve at levelIdx, a fraction of CURVE_FULL

    buildCurve()
    red, green, blue := colorUtils.rgbForCValue(rgbColor)
    repeat levelIdx from RAMP_OFF to RAMP_MAX
        factor := curve[levelIdx]
        LONG[pRamp][levelIdx] := pixels.packedCorrectedRgb(chainIdx, scaled(red, factor), scaled(green, factor), scaled(blue, factor))

PRI scaled(channel, factor) : level
' Return a channel scaled by a curve factor (a fraction of CURVE_FULL), rounded to nearest.
'
' @param channel - the color's channel at full intensity, 0 to 255
' @param factor - the curve at the intensity, a fraction of CURVE_FULL
' @returns level - the channel at that intensity

    level := (channel * factor + CURVE_HALF) / CURVE_FULL

CON ' license

